 "asked_at": "2026-10-16T12:59+08:00",
 "answer": "Singapore's air quality looks **unhealthy** right now because of smoke haze. I can't pull NEA's live feed, so these figures come from search results that were up to about a day old.\n\n- **Bernama, citing NEA, 7 pm Thursday (Oct 15):** The 24-hour PSI was between 121 and 185, which is in the unhealthy range. By region it was 185 in the central region, 180 in the west, 160 in the east, 157 in the south and 121 in the north. One-hour PM2.5 was between 98 and 221, which is elevated to high.\n- **Cause and outlook:** The smoke is drifting in from Kalimantan. NEA said air quality could stay unhealthy or reach the lower end of \"very unhealthy\" on Friday. Winds are expected to shift to the southeast or south over the weekend, which should reduce the haze, though some smoke from Sumatra may still reach Singapore.\n- **Other trackers:** One showed a 24-hour PSI of 175 in the west and 1-hour PM2.5 of 234 at 5 pm. Another listed 24-hour PSI of 153–226, which would be very unhealthy. These numbers don't match each other, and they may be from different times.\n\n**What to do:**\n- Reduce prolonged or strenuous outdoor activity, especially if you're elderly, pregnant, a child, or have heart or lung conditions.\n- If you do go out, an N95 mask works better than a surgical mask.\n- Use the one-hour PM2.5 reading to decide on short-term plans like a run. The 24-hour PSI is better for the overall picture.\n\nFor the exact current numbers, check NEA's site (haze.gov.sg) or the myENV app, which update hourly.",
